-- title:
--   Translating a cone along its axis by $\varrho/\sin\vartheta$ lands inside its $\varrho$-shrinking
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a real inner product space, let $v \in E$ be a unit vector, let $\vartheta$ satisfy $0 < \vartheta \le \pi/2$, and let $\varrho \ge 0$. Write
--   $$\tilde V = \tilde V(v,\vartheta) = \{\,h \ne 0 : \langle v,h\rangle/\|h\| > \cos\vartheta\,\}$$
--   for the cone with axis $v$ and apex angle $\vartheta$, write $S[a] = a + S$ for the translate of a set $S$ by $a$, and write
--   $$S_\varrho \;=\; \{\,y \in S : \bar B_\varrho(y) \subseteq S\,\}$$
--   for the $\varrho$-shrinking of $S$, the set of points of $S$ whose closed $\varrho$-ball is still contained in $S$. Then
--   $$\tilde V\Bigl[\tfrac{\varrho}{\sin\vartheta}\,v\Bigr] \;\subseteq\; \tilde V_{\varrho} .$$
--
--   In words: pushing the whole cone a distance $\varrho/\sin\vartheta$ along its own axis produces a cone every point of which has clearance at least $\varrho$ inside the original. This is the quantitative form of "a shifted cone is a half-cone", and it is how a set defined by a strict cone condition acquires a safety margin large enough to absorb perturbations of size $\varrho$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/dbenbenn/quadratic-forms-sobolev/blob/7a1a680db2124d46ce370c91fd450aa454edf491/QuadraticFormsSobolev/ConeGap.lean#L195-L202

